===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|id=beginning|enm:stert<id:start>|tree=1}}
From {{der|en|enm|stert}}, from the verb {{m|enm|sterten||to start, startle}}. See below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The [[beginning]] of an activity.
#: {{ux|en|The movie was entertaining from '''start''' to finish.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|act=3|scene=i|passage=I see you stand like greyhounds in the slips, / Straining upon the '''start'''.}}
# A sudden involuntary [[movement]].
#: {{ux|en|He woke with a '''start'''.}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=Nature does nothing by '''starts''' and leaps, or in a hurry.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1885|author=w:Robert Louis Stevenson|title={{w|Olalla (short story)|Olalla}}
|passage=The sight of his scared face, his '''starts''' and pallors and sudden harkenings, unstrung me {{...}}}}
# The beginning [[point]] of a [[race]], a [[board game]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|Captured pieces are returned to the '''start''' of the board.}}
# An [[appearance]] in a sports game, horserace, etc., from the beginning of the event.
#: {{ux|en|Jones has been a substitute before, but made his first '''start''' for the team last Sunday.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 12, 2011| author=Ian Hughes| title=Arsenal 2 - 0 Wolverhampton| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9391038.stm| passage=Wilshere, who made his first '''start''' for England in the midweek friendly win over Denmark, raced into the penalty area and chose to cross rather than shoot - one of the very few poor selections he made in the match.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260922030041/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9391038.stm|archivedate=22 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|horticulture}} A young [[plant]] [[germinate]]d in a pot to be [[transplant]]ed later.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Liz Primeau; Steven A. Frowine|title=Gardening Basics For Canadians For Dummies
|passage=You generally see nursery '''starts''' at garden centres in mid to late spring. Small annual plants are generally sold in four-packs or larger packs, with each cell holding a single young plant.}}
# An initial advantage over somebody else; a [[head start]].
#: {{ux|en|to get, or have, the '''start'''}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Max Carrados|passage=The man has got two clear days' '''start''' and the chances are nine to one against catching him.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang|archaic}} A [[happening]] or [[proceeding]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=Hawley Smart|title=A False Start|volume=2|page=69
|passage=“It's a rum '''start''', old John Madingley's coming down to Tunnleton,” said Grafton, one evening in the smoking-room; {{...}}}}
